    "q-134079": {
      "dom_id": "step-q134079",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $6$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Education, in years ($y$)** | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages and corresponding years of formal education completed by each of Mark's cousins.\nCompute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=11$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(6-11)(2-6)+(10-11)(4-6)+(12-11)(8-6)+(16-11)(10-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-5)(-4)+(-1)(-2)+(1)(2)+(5)(4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[20+2+2+20] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 44 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
